2b:T1b09,<p>A lot of people give up on salads because an hour later they're hungry again. That isn't a salad problem — it's a building problem. A <strong>balanced salad bowl</strong> needs more than leaves. When we design a bowl at Leafli, we check it against the same six building blocks every time.</p>
<h2 id="the-six-parts-of-a-balanced-salad-bowl">The six parts of a balanced salad bowl</h2><h3 id="1-a-generous-base-of-vegetables">1. A generous base of vegetables</h3><p>Leafy greens, crunchy vegetables and a little fruit give you fibre, vitamins and volume for very few calories. The World Health Organization recommends eating <a href="https://www.who.int/news-room/fact-sheets/detail/healthy-diet" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">at least 400 g of fruit and vegetables a day</a> — a good salad can cover a big share of that in one meal.</p>
<p><strong>Tip:</strong> mix textures. Crisp lettuce, juicy cherry tomatoes and shredded purple cabbage feel more satisfying than one kind of leaf.</p>
<h3 id="2-a-real-portion-of-protein">2. A real portion of protein</h3><p>Protein is what turns a side salad into lunch. It slows digestion and helps you stay full. Good options:</p>
<ul>
<li>Chickpeas, moong sprouts or other legumes</li>
<li>Paneer, mozzarella or hung curd</li>
<li>Eggs</li>
<li>Grilled chicken</li>
</ul>
<p>Our <a href="/menu/desi-mango-sprout-salad">Desi Mango &amp; Sprout Salad</a> uses both chana and moong, while the <a href="/menu/classic-chicken-caesar-salad">Classic Chicken Caesar</a> pairs grilled chicken with eggs.</p>
<h3 id="3-some-slow-release-carbohydrates">3. Some slow-release carbohydrates</h3><p>A small portion of grains, noodles, potatoes or legumes gives you steady energy for the afternoon. Think roasted potatoes, rice noodles or extra chana — enough to fuel you, not so much that you get sleepy.</p>
<h3 id="4-healthy-fats">4. Healthy fats</h3><p>Fat carries flavour and helps your body absorb fat-soluble vitamins from the vegetables. Nuts, seeds, a cold-pressed oil dressing, avocado or cheese all count. A little goes a long way — we weigh ours to the gram.</p>
<h3 id="5-something-crunchy">5. Something crunchy</h3><p>Crunch makes a bowl satisfying to eat. Toasted seeds, peanuts, walnuts, croutons or granola all work. Add them at the last minute so they stay crisp — which is why our crunchy toppings travel separately.</p>
<h3 id="6-a-dressing-with-personality">6. A dressing with personality</h3><p>A good dressing ties everything together. Bright acidity (lemon, vinegar), something savoury, and a touch of sweetness or heat. Keep it on the side until you're ready to eat so your greens don't wilt.</p>
<h2 id="a-balanced-salad-bowl-by-the-numbers">A balanced salad bowl, by the numbers</h2><p>There's no single perfect ratio, but this is a useful starting point for one bowl:</p>
<table>
<thead>
<tr>
<th>Part</th>
<th>Roughly</th>
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody><tr>
<td>Vegetables &amp; greens</td>
<td>Half the bowl</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Protein</td>
<td>A quarter of the bowl</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Slow carbs</td>
<td>A small handful</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Healthy fats &amp; crunch</td>
<td>1–2 tablespoons</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td>Dressing</td>
<td>2–3 tablespoons, on the side</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table>
<h2 id="common-mistakes-that-leave-you-hungry">Common mistakes that leave you hungry</h2><ul>
<li><strong>Leaves only.</strong> Without protein and some carbs, a salad won't last you till evening.</li>
<li><strong>Drowning it in dressing.</strong> Too much turns a fresh bowl heavy. Start with less; you can always add more.</li>
<li><strong>Dressing it too early.</strong> Pre-dressed salads go soggy fast. Toss just before eating.</li>
<li><strong>No variety.</strong> Rotating your bowls through the week keeps it interesting — and gives you a wider range of nutrients.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="three-balanced-salad-bowl-ideas-to-try-this-week">Three balanced salad bowl ideas to try this week</h2><p><strong>The desi protein bowl.</strong> Chana and moong sprouts for protein, cucumber, tomato and onion for crunch, diced mango for sweetness, and a squeeze of lemon with chaat masala. Finish with a tangy mango-chilli dressing on the side. It's light, but the legumes keep you going all afternoon.</p>
<p><strong>The Mediterranean-style bowl.</strong> A bed of rocket and lettuce, slices of tomato and fresh mozzarella, plenty of basil, a few toasted pine nuts and a basil-walnut pesto. The cheese brings protein and fat, so a modest portion is satisfying.</p>
<p><strong>The warm-and-cool bowl.</strong> Roasted potatoes (cooled), crisp apple, lettuce, cherry tomatoes and walnuts, with a creamy, tangy egg-based dressing. The potatoes cover your slow carbs and the walnuts add healthy fats.</p>
<p>Each of these hits all six parts of a balanced salad bowl — vegetables, protein, slow carbs, healthy fats, crunch and dressing — in a different way, so you can rotate them without getting bored.</p>
<h2 id="adjusting-your-bowl-for-different-goals">Adjusting your bowl for different goals</h2><ul>
<li><strong>More energy for workouts:</strong> add an extra portion of slow carbs such as noodles, potatoes or legumes.</li>
<li><strong>Feeling hungry by 4 pm:</strong> increase the protein first, then the healthy fats.</li>
<li><strong>Lighter dinner:</strong> keep the vegetables generous and go easy on carbs and dressing.</li>
<li><strong>Managing a health condition:</strong> portions matter more than rules — check with a doctor or registered dietitian.</li>
</ul>
<h2 id="building-a-balanced-salad-bowl-on-a-budget">Building a balanced salad bowl on a budget</h2><p>Eating well doesn't need imported ingredients. Some of the best-value building blocks are already in an Indian kitchen:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Seasonal vegetables</strong> — cabbage, carrot, cucumber, tomato and onion are cheap, crunchy and last well.</li>
<li><strong>Dried legumes</strong> — a kilo of dried chana or moong makes many portions of protein once soaked and cooked.</li>
<li><strong>Peanuts and sesame</strong> — affordable crunch and healthy fats; toast them for more flavour.</li>
<li><strong>Homemade dressings</strong> — lemon, curd, mint, cumin and a little oil cost a fraction of bottled dressings.</li>
<li><strong>Seasonal fruit</strong> — mango in summer, oranges and guava in winter add sweetness for very little.</li>
</ul>
<p>Buy whole vegetables rather than pre-cut, cook legumes in batches, and plan bowls around what's in season.</p>
<h2 id="make-it-easy-to-stick-with">Make it easy to stick with</h2><p>The hardest part of eating well is doing it every day. If you'd rather not plan and chop, a <a href="/plans">salad subscription</a> delivers a balanced bowl on the days you choose, with dressings packed separately so it arrives crisp.</p>
<p>Start with a few bowls a week, notice how you feel in the afternoons, and build from there.</p>
